  "note": "Triennial election, Thursday 15 January 1914. Eleven candidates were nominated for the nine seats and John Anderson withdrew, leaving ten (Armagh Guardian): the old members Coburn, Jackson, Meredith, Neill, Roberts, Crothers and White, and the new candidates Adair, Aston and Davison. The paper heads its list \"First nine elected\" and marks Coburn, Roberts, Meredith, Jackson, Crothers, White, Neill and Adair \"(a)\", which it does not explain. No party labels are printed. Boyd Coburn: no party label printed in 1914. At other elections he stood as Unionist (Tandragee, 15 January 1908, printed 'U.': Northern Whig, 17 January 1908, p. 8 (\"Tandragee\"); also the Irish News, 16 January 1908, p. 3 (\"Tandragee\"); Tandragee, 15 January 1920, listed as Unionist: Portadown Express, 23 January 1920, p. 5 (\"Majority of old members returned at Tandragee\"); withdrawals from the Lurgan Mail, 17 January 1920, p. 5). William John Meredith: no party label printed in 1914. At other elections he stood as Unionist (Tandragee, 15 January 1908, printed 'U.': Northern Whig, 17 January 1908, p. 8 (\"Tandragee\"); also the Irish News, 16 January 1908, p. 3 (\"Tandragee\"); Tandragee, 15 January 1920, listed as Unionist: Portadown Express, 23 January 1920, p. 5 (\"Majority of old members returned at Tandragee\"); withdrawals from the Lurgan Mail, 17 January 1920, p. 5). Thomas Neill: no party label printed in 1914. At other elections he stood as Unionist (Tandragee, 15 January 1908, printed 'U.': Northern Whig, 17 January 1908, p. 8 (\"Tandragee\"); also the Irish News, 16 January 1908, p. 3 (\"Tandragee\"); Tandragee, 15 January 1920, listed as Unionist: Portadown Express, 23 January 1920, p. 5 (\"Majority of old members returned at Tandragee\"); withdrawals from the Lurgan Mail, 17 January 1920, p. 5)."
